Isolated Ti/Zr QH diagnostic run on thu-GenSi.

Command (one process per system):
  /root/miniconda3/envs/mat-agent/bin/python -m fes_bench.qh.run --config /tmp/{ti,zr}_qh_domains_alloy.json

Remote output root:
  /GenSIvePFS/users/zirenj/fes-bench/runs/phase2_tizr_qh_20260921/results

Ti completed with 693 temperatures per phase.  Domains_Alloy minimum
frequencies by volume were hcp [0.44850, 0.45482, 0.45653] THz and bcc
[-4.23604, -4.34294, -4.45902] THz.  Therefore Ti hcp is
qh_reliable=true and Ti bcc is qh_reliable=false.  The bcc imaginary modes
are expected and were not suppressed.

The run uses the existing diagnostic protocol (2x2x2 supercell, 12x12x12
mesh, three volume scales).  It is explicitly not claimed as the final
five-volume / >=10-A / >=15-per-A production protocol; that deviation is
recorded in each phonon_report.json.

Zr completed with 910 temperatures per phase.  Domains_Alloy minimum
frequencies by volume were hcp [0.28008, 0.28511, 0.28652] THz and bcc
[-1.95664, -2.15484, -2.35690] THz.  Therefore Zr hcp is
qh_reliable=true and Zr bcc is qh_reliable=false.  The bcc imaginary modes
are expected and were not suppressed.

Both systems wrote `fqh.csv` and `qh_summary.json` under the isolated remote
run root; the CSVs were copied into the canonical local phase directories and
the per-phase reliability reports preserve the remote provenance.
